  "query_preview": "/Through Sunday/...\nIssued at 1105 PM MDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nLooking at water vapor on satellite, the upper level trough we've \nbeen talking about for the past few days is finally moving onto \nthe West Coast. Ahead of this deep trough, southwesterly upper \nlevel winds are funneling in moisture from Post-Tropical Cyclone \nOdalys into our area. At lower levels overnight, south/southeast \nsurface winds will continue to increase moisture at the surface. \nThis increase in moisture combined with upper level lift ahead of\nthe approaching trough will bring a round of showers and storms \nMonday afternoon and evening. Showers are forecast to move into \nour area from the southwest, progressing northeast through the \nevening. This activity is expected to be primarily rain showers, \ndropping anywhere from a few hundredths to around half an inch of \nrain. However, we could see a few storms develop in the later \nafternoon and evening, which could produce some locally heavy \nrainfall exceeding an inch.\n\nRain chances will continue on and off through at least Thursday \nmorning as that upper trough approaches and then sits just to the\nsoutheast of our area. A cold front will move through early \nTuesday, bringing slightly cooler temperatures and the potential \nfor surface convergence leading to a band of some heavier precip \nsometime Tuesday morning. QG forcing from the upper low and \nPWs are forecast to peak on Wednesday. This is the day we're most\nconcerned about. There's still significant discrepancies on where\nthe axis of heavier precip will form (which is expected), with\nmodels in pretty good agreement that there will be a swath of \narea that sees 3+ inches with this event. We will likely be right\nat the northern edge of this swath, meaning it could stay just to\nour south and east or portions of our area could see very heavy\nrainfall and flash flooding. Right now, Denver is sitting at a \nmedium chance (~40%) of seeing at least an inch of rain on \nWednesday, with chances increasing as we go southeast. Lincoln \ncounty is the most likely area in our CWA to see the higher \nrainfall totals, with a 60% to 70% chance of at least an inch on \nWednesday.\n\nThe forecast precipitable water values for Tuesday, Wednesday, \nand potentially Thursday are VERY high. Models are showing \nwidespread PWs of 200% to 250% of normal, with potential for it \nto reach 300% of normal. With this much moisture, any shower or storm\nthat can get going will have the potential to produce very heavy \nrainfall and flash flooding. So, even if we get missed with the \nmain swath of heavy rain and don't receive the widespread heavy \nrainfall across our area, there still will be a very real threat \nfor localized flash flooding for any area that ends up with a \nstorm. \n\nAs for mountain snow chances, temperatures still look very \nmarginal, which does not bode well for snowfall totals. Areas\nabove 12,000ft will likely see at least a round or two of snow \nflurries in the Tuesday through Thursday time period (best chance\nWed and Thu mornings), leading to a dusting of snow for those \nareas above 12,000ft. But chances for much more than that are low\nat this time. Rain chances will start to decrease on Thursday as \nthe upper level system moves off to the east. Temperatures Friday \nthrough the weekend will warm closer to normal.",
